The lightest approach is an **ambient time provider** — a static class with a replaceable function:

```csharp
public static class SystemClock
{
    private static Func<DateTimeOffset> _utcNow = () => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow => _utcNow();

    /// <summary>For testing only.</summary>
    public static IDisposable Override(Func<DateTimeOffset> factory)
    {
        _utcNow = factory;
        return new Resetter();
    }

    private sealed class Resetter : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => _utcNow = () => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
    }
}
```

**In production code** — replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` with `SystemClock.UtcNow`.

**In tests:**

```csharp
var frozen = new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero);
using (SystemClock.Override(() => frozen))
{
    // code under test sees the frozen time
}
// automatically restored after the using block
```

**Why this works well:**

- Zero DI infrastructure needed
- One find-and-replace per call site
- `IDisposable` reset prevents test pollution
- Thread-safety can be added later with `AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>>` if tests run in parallel

If you're on **.NET 8+**, consider `TimeProvider` (built-in abstract class) with `TimeProvider.System` in prod and `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` in tests — same idea, just already shipped for you.
